You are extracting regulatory coding rules for ONE assessment item from an official source document, for a rulebook that nurses and state auditors will rely on. The rulebook is only useful if every statement in it is true to the document and traceable to a page. A confidently wrong rule is the worst possible outcome; an honest NOT_FOUND is a good outcome.

Rules you must follow:

1. Never invent a rule. Only record what the supplied pages state about this item. Do not fill gaps from general knowledge, other manuals, or what "usually" applies. If the pages do not say it, it is NOT_FOUND.
2. Citations or it doesn't exist. Every rule, coding level, documentation criterion, state delta, conflict position and audit citation carries a `source` with `doc` = "<%= doc_id %>" and `loc` = the page it came from, written exactly as `p. N` using the N from the `[PAGE N]` marker above the text<% if attachment %> (or the printed page number of the attached document when no markers are present)<% end %>. Include a short verbatim `quote` from that page for each citation whenever possible.
3. NOT_FOUND over inference. If the lookback window is not stated, set `lookback_days` to null, explain in `lookback_notes`, and add "lookback_days" to `not_found`. Do the same for any other field you cannot source: leave it empty or null and name it in `not_found`.
4. Preserve conflicts. If two passages disagree (or a passage disagrees with a table), record both positions with their own citations in `conflicts`, and set `resolution` to "unresolved — surface to user" unless the document itself resolves it.
5. Stay inside the document. Do not attribute state-specific rules to this document unless the document itself is a state document that states them. `supportive_documentation.state` is always `{}` in this step.
6. `verified_by_expert` is always false in this step; a human reviewer sets it later. `audit_weight` reflects how the document itself frames the criterion (required documentation = high; suggested/example = low); say "medium" when unclear.
7. `case_mix_relevance.level` is "unknown" unless the document explicitly ties this item to a payment/case-mix classification; when it does, cite it.
8. Return only the JSON object described by the schema below. No prose, no markdown fences.

